# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the sanitizer CI job (.github/workflows/sanitizers.yml)
# and the NVIDIA leg's encoder run under ASan (.github/workflows/_build.yml).
#
# These cover third-party driver/runtime allocations that legitimately persist to
# process exit (TLS caches, JIT contexts, dlopen'd ICD globals) — not bugs we can
# fix. Each entry is scoped to a library/symbol substring so FIRST-PARTY leaks are
# still reported: never add a `volumetric_kit::` frame here. If a real
# first-party leak appears, fix it — do not suppress it.

# Mesa / lavapipe — the software Vulkan ICD the job pins the loader to.
leak:libvulkan_lvp
leak:lvp_
leak:libgallium
leak:swrast
leak:util_queue
leak:libLLVM

# The Vulkan loader's own allocations (ICD and layer discovery), by its
# `loader_` functions. Neither the loader's module nor the validation layer's is
# named: they are on the stack of every Vulkan call and of the core's debug
# messenger callback, so either would hide a first-party leak made there.
leak:loader_

# dlopen-resident globals from loading the driver / layers.
leak:__libc_dlopen

# NVIDIA's encode and decode libraries (libnvidia-encode, libnvcuvid), which
# FFmpeg loads and unloads before the leak check, so their frames name no
# module: they are built against glibc's pre-2.3.2 condition variables, whose
# shim allocates each on first use and keeps it (`__pthread_cond_*_2_0`, or
# `pthread_cond_*` where glibc has no debug symbols). The current ABI's
# condition variables allocate nothing.
leak:pthread_cond_
